{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_psych_interventions_meta_2024_0001", "source_registry_id": "src_sport_psychology_psych_interventions_meta_2024", "source_file": "psychological_interventions_sport_performance_meta_2024.url", "source_path": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10933186/", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10933186/", "source_label": "Psychological interventions for sports performance systematic review and meta-analysis", "quality_tier": "systematic_review_meta_analysis", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["sport psychology", "psychological intervention", "performance", "mental skills"], "text": "Psychological interventions for sport performance should be treated as explanatory mental-skills support. The review evidence supports using structured psychological techniques to influence performance-related outcomes, but these techniques do not authorize changes to running volume, intensity, injury decisions, or nutrition prescriptions. Use for confidence, attention, coping, and preparation notes only."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_self_talk_imagery_goal_setting_2024_0002", "source_registry_id": "src_sport_psychology_psych_interventions_meta_2024", "source_file": "psychological_interventions_sport_performance_meta_2024.url", "source_path": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10933186/", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10933186/", "source_label": "Psychological interventions for sports performance systematic review and meta-analysis", "quality_tier": "systematic_review_meta_analysis", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["self-talk", "imagery", "goal setting", "mental skills", "visualization"], "text": "Common mental-skills categories in sport psychology include self-talk, imagery or visualization, goal setting, relaxation or arousal regulation, and attentional strategies. In a marathon assistant these can be surfaced as brief coping scripts, process-goal reminders, or race-preparation prompts, while remaining separate from planner and executor training prescriptions."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_ioc_mental_health_boundary_2019_0003", "source_registry_id": "src_ioc_mental_health_elite_athletes_2019", "source_file": "ioc_consensus_mental_health_elite_athletes_2019.url", "source_path": "https://bjsm.bmj.com/content/53/11/667", "source_url": "https://bjsm.bmj.com/content/53/11/667", "source_label": "IOC consensus statement on mental health in elite athletes", "quality_tier": "consensus_statement", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["mental health", "athlete", "clinical boundary", "referral", "psychology"], "text": "The IOC consensus statement frames athlete mental health as a clinical and safeguarding domain when symptoms are persistent, severe, or impairing. The psychologist role in this system must not diagnose anxiety, depression, or other clinical states; it should provide non-clinical sport psychology references and recommend professional mental-health support when distress is ongoing or outside training-preparation scope."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_goal_setting_oxford_2019_0004", "source_registry_id": "src_goal_setting_sport_performance_oxford_2019", "source_file": "goal_setting_sport_performance_oxford_2019.url", "source_path": "https://oxfordre.com/psychology/display/10.1093/acrefore/9780190236557.001.0001/acrefore-9780190236557-e-152", "source_url": "https://oxfordre.com/psychology/display/10.1093/acrefore/9780190236557.001.0001/acrefore-9780190236557-e-152", "source_label": "Goal Setting and Performance in Sport and Exercise Settings", "quality_tier": "expert_review", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["goal setting", "process goals", "performance goals", "motivation"], "text": "Goal-setting literature in sport and exercise distinguishes outcome, performance, and process goals. For running plans, the psychology role should favor process goals and controllable execution cues because they support adherence and focus without altering the training prescription generated by planner and executor."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_attentional_focus_framework_1977_0005", "source_registry_id": "src_attentional_focus_endurance_running_review_2014", "source_file": "attentional_focus_endurance_running_review_2014.url", "source_path": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Associative-and-dissociative-cognitive-strategies-Masters-Ogles/0b6318c8ff819907d9a8f3412d13fa10c9342c97", "source_url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Associative-and-dissociative-cognitive-strategies-Masters-Ogles/0b6318c8ff819907d9a8f3412d13fa10c9342c97", "source_label": "Associative and Dissociative Cognitive Strategies in Exercise and Running: 20 Years Later, What Do We Know? — Masters & Ogles (1998)", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_review", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["attentional focus", "associative", "dissociative", "marathon", "pacing"], "text": "Morgan and Pollock (1977) introduced the foundational distinction between associative and dissociative attentional strategies in marathon runners. Elite runners predominantly used associative strategies — monitoring bodily sensations such as breathing rate, muscular tension, and perceived effort — while non-elite runners more often used dissociative strategies to distract themselves from discomfort. In a marathon assistant context, this research supports offering attention-management cues that match the runner's training stage and experience level."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_attentional_focus_elite_vs_novice_0006", "source_registry_id": "src_attentional_focus_endurance_running_review_2014", "source_file": "attentional_focus_endurance_running_review_2014.url", "source_path": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Associative-and-dissociative-cognitive-strategies-Masters-Ogles/0b6318c8ff819907d9a8f3412d13fa10c9342c97", "source_url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Associative-and-dissociative-cognitive-strategies-Masters-Ogles/0b6318c8ff819907d9a8f3412d13fa10c9342c97", "source_label": "Associative and Dissociative Cognitive Strategies in Exercise and Running: 20 Years Later, What Do We Know? — Masters & Ogles (1998)", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_review", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["attentional focus", "elite runners", "novice runners", "experience", "pacing"], "text": "Research consistently finds that attentional strategy use is related more to running experience than to sex or baseline fitness. Experienced recreational runners are more adept at diverting attention away from unpleasant bodily cues, while inexperienced runners report more focus on internal body sensations and find this monitoring distressing rather than useful. The psychologist node should calibrate its attention-management suggestions to the runner's stated experience level in their profile."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_attentional_focus_performance_link_0007", "source_registry_id": "src_attentional_focus_endurance_running_review_2014", "source_file": "attentional_focus_endurance_running_review_2014.url", "source_path": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Associative-and-dissociative-cognitive-strategies-Masters-Ogles/0b6318c8ff819907d9a8f3412d13fa10c9342c97", "source_url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Associative-and-dissociative-cognitive-strategies-Masters-Ogles/0b6318c8ff819907d9a8f3412d13fa10c9342c97", "source_label": "Associative and Dissociative Cognitive Strategies in Exercise and Running: 20 Years Later, What Do We Know? — Masters & Ogles (1998)", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_review", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 3, "keywords_en": ["attentional focus", "performance", "pacing", "RPE", "running economy"], "text": "Multiple studies report that associative attentional strategies correlate with faster marathon performance because continuous monitoring of perceived effort enables more accurate real-time pacing. Dissociative strategies correlate with lower rating of perceived exertion (RPE) and can improve running economy in the short term, but may lead to pacing errors over long distances. For runners targeting a specific finish time, associative attention cues may be more beneficial in the final race phase."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_attentional_focus_external_novice_2016_0008", "source_registry_id": "src_frontiers_attentional_focus_endurance_2019", "source_file": "frontiers_attentional_focus_endurance_2019.url", "source_path": "https://frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2019.00535/full", "source_url": "https://frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2019.00535/full", "source_label": "Action Monitoring Through External or Internal Focus of Attention Does Not Impair Endurance Performance — Frontiers in Psychology 2019", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["external focus", "dissociative", "novice runners", "running economy", "attention"], "text": "A ScienceDirect (2016) study found that for inexperienced runners, directing attention to an external environmental target — scenery, music, a point ahead — produced the lowest oxygen consumption and RPE compared with focusing on breathing or stride mechanics. The finding challenges the assumption that beginners should focus on form cues; for early-stage runners, external dissociative attention may reduce perceived effort and sustain motivation. Marathon assistant psychologist suggestions should reflect this distinction when the runner is in early base-building phases."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_attentional_focus_two_dimensions_1998_0009", "source_registry_id": "src_attentional_focus_endurance_running_review_2014", "source_file": "attentional_focus_endurance_running_review_2014.url", "source_path": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Associative-and-dissociative-cognitive-strategies-Masters-Ogles/0b6318c8ff819907d9a8f3412d13fa10c9342c97", "source_url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Associative-and-dissociative-cognitive-strategies-Masters-Ogles/0b6318c8ff819907d9a8f3412d13fa10c9342c97", "source_label": "Associative and Dissociative Cognitive Strategies in Exercise and Running: 20 Years Later, What Do We Know? — Masters & Ogles (1998)", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_review", "page": 2, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["attentional focus", "internal", "external", "associative", "dissociative", "taxonomy"], "text": "Stevinson and Biddle (1998) proposed a two-dimensional taxonomy of attentional focus in running: internal vs. external, and task-related vs. non-task-related. This refined the original associative/dissociative binary and better captures how runners actually shift their attention during a race. Practical application: a runner might use internal-associative focus (monitoring leg fatigue) in final miles but benefit from external-dissociative focus (crowd energy) when motivation is flagging at mid-race."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_attentional_focus_chunking_race_0010", "source_registry_id": "src_attentional_focus_endurance_running_review_2014", "source_file": "attentional_focus_endurance_running_review_2014.url", "source_path": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Associative-and-dissociative-cognitive-strategies-Masters-Ogles/0b6318c8ff819907d9a8f3412d13fa10c9342c97", "source_url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/Associative-and-dissociative-cognitive-strategies-Masters-Ogles/0b6318c8ff819907d9a8f3412d13fa10c9342c97", "source_label": "Associative and Dissociative Cognitive Strategies in Exercise and Running: 20 Years Later, What Do We Know? — Masters & Ogles (1998)", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_review", "page": 2, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["attentional focus", "chunking", "race segmentation", "distance management", "pacing strategy"], "text": "A practical application of attentional focus research for marathon runners is race segmentation or 'chunking': mentally dividing the race into smaller, manageable units and applying specific attentional strategies to each segment. Elite runners commonly adopt an associative internal focus for the first half to establish correct pacing, then shift to external dissociative strategies in the difficult middle miles, and return to internal associative monitoring in the final 10 km. This tiered approach is supported by applied sport psychology literature."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_attentional_focus_practical_cues_0011", "source_registry_id": "src_frontiers_attentional_focus_endurance_2019", "source_file": "frontiers_attentional_focus_endurance_2019.url", "source_path": "https://frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2019.00535/full", "source_url": "https://frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2019.00535/full", "source_label": "Action Monitoring Through External or Internal Focus of Attention Does Not Impair Endurance Performance — Frontiers in Psychology 2019", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["attentional cues", "running form", "breath", "rhythm", "focus keyword"], "text": "Practical attention-focus cues for marathon runners include: (1) associative cues — 'check in with your breathing every 10 minutes', 'scan for unnecessary tension in shoulders and hands', 'notice your cadence'; (2) external dissociative cues — 'look at the horizon rather than your feet', 'use crowd energy as fuel in the final 5 km'. The psychologist node should offer these as brief preparation scripts or in-training reminders, not as instructions to modify pace or volume."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_attentional_focus_mental_fatigue_0012", "source_registry_id": "src_frontiers_attentional_focus_endurance_2019", "source_file": "frontiers_attentional_focus_endurance_2019.url", "source_path": "https://frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2019.00535/full", "source_url": "https://frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2019.00535/full", "source_label": "Action Monitoring Through External or Internal Focus of Attention Does Not Impair Endurance Performance — Frontiers in Psychology 2019", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 2, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["mental fatigue", "attentional focus", "cognitive load", "performance decrement", "endurance"], "text": "Mental fatigue before exercise measurably reduces endurance running speed and time-to-exhaustion, independent of physiological readiness. Research demonstrates this effect operates through increased perception of effort — the brain interprets tasks as harder when cognitively depleted. Practical implication: a runner who has had a cognitively demanding workday should receive attention management suggestions that include a brief pre-session mental reset routine rather than assuming the same focus strategy will work every day."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_self_talk_endurance_overview_2014_0013", "source_registry_id": "src_selftalk_endurance_blanchfield_2014", "source_file": "selftalk_endurance_blanchfield_2014.url", "source_path": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24121242/", "source_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24121242/", "source_label": "Talking yourself out of exhaustion: the effects of self-talk on endurance performance — Blanchfield et al. 2014, MSSE", "quality_tier": "randomized_controlled_trial", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["self-talk", "endurance", "perceived effort", "performance", "fatigue"], "text": "Blanchfield et al. (2014) conducted a randomized controlled trial demonstrating that motivational self-talk interventions significantly improved endurance performance and time to exhaustion in a cycling task. The mechanism operates through reduced perception of effort: self-talk does not change physiological capacity but alters how difficult the effort feels. For marathon planning, the psychologist node may provide self-talk prompt suggestions per training phase or per race segment without prescribing training changes."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_self_talk_second_person_2019_0014", "source_registry_id": "src_selftalk_second_person_hardy_2019", "source_file": "selftalk_second_person_hardy_2019.url", "source_path": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31067183/", "source_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31067183/", "source_label": "To me, to you: How you say things matters for endurance performance — Hardy, Thomas & Blanchfield 2019, Journal of Sports Sciences", "quality_tier": "randomized_controlled_trial", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["self-talk", "second person", "you", "self-distancing", "endurance performance"], "text": "Hardy, Thomas, and Blanchfield (2019) found that second-person self-talk ('You can do this.') produced superior endurance outcomes compared with first-person self-talk ('I can do this.'). The mechanism is self-distancing: speaking to oneself as if from the perspective of a supportive coach reduces emotional reactivity and does not require inherent self-belief to be effective. Marathon runners in a mentally demanding training block or anxiety-prone pre-race phase may particularly benefit from scripted second-person mantras."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_self_talk_motivational_vs_instructional_0015", "source_registry_id": "src_sport_psychology_psych_interventions_meta_2024", "source_file": "psychological_interventions_sport_performance_meta_2024.url", "source_path": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10933186/", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10933186/", "source_label": "Psychological interventions for sports performance systematic review and meta-analysis — PMC 2024", "quality_tier": "systematic_review_meta_analysis", "page": 2, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["self-talk", "motivational", "instructional", "performance", "skill execution"], "text": "Sport psychology distinguishes motivational self-talk (e.g., 'Keep going, you've trained for this') from instructional self-talk (e.g., 'Relax shoulders, increase cadence'). Meta-analytic evidence shows both types improve performance, but their optimal application differs: motivational self-talk is most effective for endurance tasks requiring sustained effort, while instructional self-talk benefits technique-sensitive tasks. For marathon running, motivational self-talk is the priority application for psychologist annotations, especially during high-intensity workouts and in race contexts."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_self_talk_mantra_design_0016", "source_registry_id": "src_selftalk_endurance_blanchfield_2014", "source_file": "selftalk_endurance_blanchfield_2014.url", "source_path": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24121242/", "source_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24121242/", "source_label": "Talking yourself out of exhaustion: the effects of self-talk on endurance performance — Blanchfield et al. 2014, MSSE", "quality_tier": "randomized_controlled_trial", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["mantra", "self-talk", "simplicity", "rhythm", "personalization"], "text": "Effective marathon mantras share several evidence-informed characteristics: brevity (4–8 syllables), rhythmic alignment with cadence, positive framing (approach-oriented rather than avoidance-oriented), and personal relevance to the runner's values or race goal. Examples of approach-oriented mantras: 'Strong and steady', 'Forward motion', 'You trained for this mile'. The psychologist node should suggest 2–3 personalized mantra options based on the runner profile, particularly any stated race motivation or personal meaning they have attached to the goal."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_self_talk_negative_reframing_0017", "source_registry_id": "src_selftalk_second_person_hardy_2019", "source_file": "selftalk_second_person_hardy_2019.url", "source_path": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31067183/", "source_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31067183/", "source_label": "To me, to you: How you say things matters for endurance performance — Hardy, Thomas & Blanchfield 2019, Journal of Sports Sciences", "quality_tier": "randomized_controlled_trial", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["negative self-talk", "reframing", "cognitive restructuring", "running", "anxiety"], "text": "Negative self-talk during marathon training or racing ('I can't do this', 'My legs are dying') reliably increases perceived effort and reduces time to exhaustion. Cognitive reframing techniques involve replacing negative statements with neutral or positive alternatives — not through denial of difficulty, but through accurate reinterpretation: 'My legs feel heavy because I'm working hard — that's the training stimulus.' The psychologist node should provide per-session reframing scripts as an annotation layer on executor-generated workouts, not as instructions to modify the workout itself."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_self_talk_ultra_marathon_review_0018", "source_registry_id": "src_pain_coping_ultra_endurance_pmc_2025", "source_file": "pain_coping_ultra_endurance_pmc_2025.url", "source_path": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12476407/", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12476407/", "source_label": "Human Resilience and Pain Coping Strategies — Elite Ultra-Endurance Athletes Review, PMC 2025", "quality_tier": "systematic_review_meta_analysis", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["self-talk", "ultra marathon", "motivation", "confidence", "pain tolerance"], "text": "A systematic literature review on ultra-marathon runners found that self-talk served as a primary source of motivation during extreme fatigue states. Runners who had prepared specific self-talk scripts for anticipated low points — rather than improvising in the moment — reported greater confidence and more consistent forward movement during crisis points. This pre-planned approach transfers directly to marathon preparation: psychologist annotations should include crisis-point self-talk cues that runners can rehearse during training."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_self_talk_pain_tolerance_0019", "source_registry_id": "src_selftalk_endurance_blanchfield_2014", "source_file": "selftalk_endurance_blanchfield_2014.url", "source_path": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24121242/", "source_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24121242/", "source_label": "Talking yourself out of exhaustion: the effects of self-talk on endurance performance — Blanchfield et al. 2014, MSSE", "quality_tier": "randomized_controlled_trial", "page": 2, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["self-talk", "pain tolerance", "discomfort", "endurance", "effort perception"], "text": "Self-talk interventions have been shown to improve tolerance to exercise-induced pain and muscular discomfort, with effects mediated by improvements in self-efficacy and reduced catastrophizing. In a marathon context, runners who experienced high degrees of leg fatigue and used prepared motivational self-talk maintained higher overall performance consistency than those who did not. The psychologist node can include brief pain-management self-talk scripts as part of race-day preparation notes for runners who have flagged concern about late-race fatigue in their profile."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_self_talk_identity_0020", "source_registry_id": "src_selftalk_second_person_hardy_2019", "source_file": "selftalk_second_person_hardy_2019.url", "source_path": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31067183/", "source_url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31067183/", "source_label": "To me, to you: How you say things matters for endurance performance — Hardy, Thomas & Blanchfield 2019, Journal of Sports Sciences", "quality_tier": "randomized_controlled_trial", "page": 2, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["identity", "self-talk", "runner identity", "adherence", "motivation"], "text": "Research in motivational psychology finds that identity-based self-talk — framing oneself as 'a runner' rather than 'someone who runs' — is associated with greater long-term adherence to training. The identity framing shifts motivation from extrinsic (goal achievement) to intrinsic (expression of self), which predicts better recovery from setbacks and more consistent training behavior. Psychologist annotations during early training blocks can reinforce identity language as a structural adherence support."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_visualization_neural_basis_0021", "source_registry_id": "src_visualization_imagery_sport_performance_review", "source_file": "visualization_imagery_sport_performance_review.url", "source_path": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/344587632_Visualisation_techniques_in_sport_-_the_mental_road_map_for_success", "source_url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/344587632_Visualisation_techniques_in_sport_-_the_mental_road_map_for_success", "source_label": "Visualisation techniques in sport — the mental road map for success (ResearchGate review)", "quality_tier": "expert_review", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["visualization", "mental imagery", "neural", "motor cortex", "fMRI"], "text": "fMRI studies have demonstrated that mental imagery of athletic movement activates the same neural pathways as physical execution of that movement. This parallel activation provides the neurological basis for visualization as a training tool: systematic mental rehearsal strengthens motor programs and cognitive representations of a skill or route without physical wear. For marathon runners, visualization can be used to mentally rehearse race day without requiring additional physiological stress during taper periods."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_visualization_marathon_application_0022", "source_registry_id": "src_visualization_imagery_sport_performance_review", "source_file": "visualization_imagery_sport_performance_review.url", "source_path": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/344587632_Visualisation_techniques_in_sport_-_the_mental_road_map_for_success", "source_url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/344587632_Visualisation_techniques_in_sport_-_the_mental_road_map_for_success", "source_label": "Visualisation techniques in sport — the mental road map for success (ResearchGate review)", "quality_tier": "expert_review", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["visualization", "marathon", "race rehearsal", "course", "mental simulation"], "text": "Applied sport psychology recommends that marathon runners visualize the race in a multi-sensory manner: not just seeing the course but feeling the road surface, hearing the crowd at key points, sensing the temperature and humidity, experiencing the physical sensations of effort at each stage. A full race visualization covering the start, each major split, the halfway mental check-in, the difficult mid-race miles, and the finish should ideally take 3–5 minutes in abbreviated form. The psychologist node can include visualization scripts tailored to the runner's target race, if available in their profile."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_visualization_confidence_building_0023", "source_registry_id": "src_visualization_imagery_sport_performance_review", "source_file": "visualization_imagery_sport_performance_review.url", "source_path": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/344587632_Visualisation_techniques_in_sport_-_the_mental_road_map_for_success", "source_url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/344587632_Visualisation_techniques_in_sport_-_the_mental_road_map_for_success", "source_label": "Visualisation techniques in sport — the mental road map for success (ResearchGate review)", "quality_tier": "expert_review", "page": 2, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["visualization", "confidence", "self-efficacy", "past success", "mental rehearsal"], "text": "Visualization techniques in sport can target two distinct outcomes: performance improvement (rehearsing a skill or strategy) and confidence building (replaying successful past performances). Confidence-focused visualization involves re-experiencing previous strong training sessions or races in vivid detail to anchor the neurological and emotional memory of being capable. For runners experiencing pre-race anxiety or mid-training-block confidence dips, confidence-focused visualization scripts are a high-value psychologist annotation."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_visualization_adversity_rehearsal_0024", "source_registry_id": "src_visualization_imagery_sport_performance_review", "source_file": "visualization_imagery_sport_performance_review.url", "source_path": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/344587632_Visualisation_techniques_in_sport_-_the_mental_road_map_for_success", "source_url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/344587632_Visualisation_techniques_in_sport_-_the_mental_road_map_for_success", "source_label": "Visualisation techniques in sport — the mental road map for success (ResearchGate review)", "quality_tier": "expert_review", "page": 2, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["visualization", "adversity", "crisis rehearsal", "wall", "contingency planning"], "text": "Beyond positive performance rehearsal, adversity visualization — mentally rehearsing how to respond to anticipated difficulties such as hitting the wall, weather changes, or early cramping — is particularly valuable for marathon runners. Research on marathon runners shows that those who had mentally rehearsed specific adversity scenarios and planned their responses performed more consistently than those relying on improvised crisis management. The psychologist node may include 'what if' scenario scripts per training phase, particularly as race day approaches."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_visualization_best_practices_0025", "source_registry_id": "src_visualization_imagery_sport_performance_review", "source_file": "visualization_imagery_sport_performance_review.url", "source_path": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/344587632_Visualisation_techniques_in_sport_-_the_mental_road_map_for_success", "source_url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/344587632_Visualisation_techniques_in_sport_-_the_mental_road_map_for_success", "source_label": "Visualisation techniques in sport — the mental road map for success (ResearchGate review)", "quality_tier": "expert_review", "page": 3, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["visualization", "technique", "PETTLEP", "five senses", "mental rehearsal guide"], "text": "The PETTLEP model (Physical, Environment, Task, Timing, Learning, Emotion, Perspective) provides an evidence-based framework for high-quality mental imagery in sport. Key principles: (1) physical — adopt the body position you would be in during the activity; (2) environment — visualize in or near the actual performance environment when possible; (3) timing — use real-time duration, not speeded imagery; (4) emotion — include the emotional experience of the event, both challenge and confidence. The psychologist node should reference these principles in the brief visualization guidelines provided to runners."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_visualization_taper_period_0026", "source_registry_id": "src_visualization_imagery_sport_performance_review", "source_file": "visualization_imagery_sport_performance_review.url", "source_path": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/344587632_Visualisation_techniques_in_sport_-_the_mental_road_map_for_success", "source_url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/344587632_Visualisation_techniques_in_sport_-_the_mental_road_map_for_success", "source_label": "Visualisation techniques in sport — the mental road map for success (ResearchGate review)", "quality_tier": "expert_review", "page": 3, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["visualization", "taper", "pre-race", "mental preparation", "readiness"], "text": "During the taper period before a marathon, when physical training volume deliberately decreases, mental skills training can fill the gap in structured preparation. Sport psychology practitioners recommend increasing visualization frequency in the two weeks before a marathon, with daily mental rehearsals of the race course, start-line routine, and key race segments. This sustained mental rehearsal reduces taper anxiety and maintains neuromuscular preparation without adding physical stress. The psychologist node should flag taper-phase mental preparation as a distinct annotation block."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_visualization_injury_recovery_0027", "source_registry_id": "src_visualization_imagery_sport_performance_review", "source_file": "visualization_imagery_sport_performance_review.url", "source_path": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/344587632_Visualisation_techniques_in_sport_-_the_mental_road_map_for_success", "source_url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/344587632_Visualisation_techniques_in_sport_-_the_mental_road_map_for_success", "source_label": "Visualisation techniques in sport — the mental road map for success (ResearchGate review)", "quality_tier": "expert_review", "page": 4, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["visualization", "injury", "rehabilitation", "mental rehearsal", "return to sport"], "text": "Mental imagery has documented benefits during injury rehabilitation: athletes who use visualization during periods when physical training is restricted maintain motor skill representation and return to full performance more quickly than those relying on rest alone. Meta-analyses confirm that imagery training during rehabilitation reduces anxiety about return-to-sport and maintains movement confidence. When the therapist node identifies an injury scenario, the psychologist node can coordinate by providing visualization scripts that support rehabilitation adherence and mental readiness for return."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_visualization_outcome_vs_process_0028", "source_registry_id": "src_visualization_imagery_sport_performance_review", "source_file": "visualization_imagery_sport_performance_review.url", "source_path": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/344587632_Visualisation_techniques_in_sport_-_the_mental_road_map_for_success", "source_url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/344587632_Visualisation_techniques_in_sport_-_the_mental_road_map_for_success", "source_label": "Visualisation techniques in sport — the mental road map for success (ResearchGate review)", "quality_tier": "expert_review", "page": 4, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["visualization", "outcome goals", "process goals", "focus", "imagery type"], "text": "Sport psychology differentiates between outcome imagery (visualizing a finish time or placing) and process imagery (visualizing correct execution of pace, breathing, and form). Research suggests process imagery produces greater performance benefits for complex endurance tasks because it reinforces controllable behaviors rather than outcome uncertainty. For marathon runners, psychologist visualization scripts should emphasize process imagery — the feeling of running smoothly, the experience of maintaining pace — rather than exclusively visualizing the finish time or placement."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_goal_setting_theory_locke_latham_0029", "source_registry_id": "src_goal_setting_sport_exercise_locke_latham_2002", "source_file": "goal_setting_sport_exercise_locke_latham_2002.url", "source_path": "https://med.stanford.edu/content/dam/sm/s-spire/documents/PD.locke-and-latham-retrospective_Paper.pdf", "source_url": "https://med.stanford.edu/content/dam/sm/s-spire/documents/PD.locke-and-latham-retrospective_Paper.pdf", "source_label": "Building a Practically Useful Theory of Goal Setting and Task Motivation — Locke & Latham 2002, American Psychologist", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["goal setting", "Locke", "Latham", "specific goals", "challenging goals", "motivation"], "text": "Locke and Latham's goal-setting theory (1990, 2002) establishes that specific and challenging goals produce higher performance than vague ('do your best') goals. The theory identifies five key goal-setting principles: clarity, challenge, commitment, feedback, and task complexity. In marathon training, psychologist annotations can reference these principles when reminding runners why their prescribed training targets serve a purpose, and why tracking completion matters for motivational coherence."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_goal_types_outcome_performance_process_0030", "source_registry_id": "src_goal_setting_sport_exercise_locke_latham_2002", "source_file": "goal_setting_sport_exercise_locke_latham_2002.url", "source_path": "https://med.stanford.edu/content/dam/sm/s-spire/documents/PD.locke-and-latham-retrospective_Paper.pdf", "source_url": "https://med.stanford.edu/content/dam/sm/s-spire/documents/PD.locke-and-latham-retrospective_Paper.pdf", "source_label": "Building a Practically Useful Theory of Goal Setting and Task Motivation — Locke & Latham 2002, American Psychologist", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["goal types", "outcome goal", "performance goal", "process goal", "marathon", "adherence"], "text": "Sport psychology distinguishes three goal types: outcome goals (finish sub-3:30), performance goals (achieve negative splits in the long run), and process goals (maintain 170 bpm cadence for the first 30 km). Research shows that athletes who rely exclusively on outcome goals are more vulnerable to motivation collapse when conditions change, while athletes with strong process goal structures remain more consistent. The psychologist node should emphasize process goals in its annotations, particularly for runners in volatile training phases where outcome targets may shift."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_goal_setting_tiered_marathon_0031", "source_registry_id": "src_goal_setting_self_regulation_marathon_2023", "source_file": "goal_setting_self_regulation_marathon_2023.url", "source_path": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1469029223001401", "source_url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1469029223001401", "source_label": "Self-regulatory processes in goal striving during excellent distance-running performances — ScienceDirect 2023", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["tiered goals", "goal hierarchy", "A goal", "B goal", "C goal", "marathon flexibility"], "text": "Applied sport psychology for marathon racing recommends a tiered goal structure: an A goal (ideal performance under perfect conditions), a B goal (solid performance given moderate challenge), and a C goal (completion or execution of the plan regardless of time). Research on self-regulatory processes during excellent distance-running performances found that athletes who had pre-committed to tiered goals made faster and more effective in-race adjustments when their A goal became unachievable. The psychologist node can surface tiered goal reminders in race-week preparation notes."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_goal_setting_SMART_0032", "source_registry_id": "src_goal_setting_sport_exercise_locke_latham_2002", "source_file": "goal_setting_sport_exercise_locke_latham_2002.url", "source_path": "https://med.stanford.edu/content/dam/sm/s-spire/documents/PD.locke-and-latham-retrospective_Paper.pdf", "source_url": "https://med.stanford.edu/content/dam/sm/s-spire/documents/PD.locke-and-latham-retrospective_Paper.pdf", "source_label": "Building a Practically Useful Theory of Goal Setting and Task Motivation — Locke & Latham 2002, American Psychologist", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 2, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["SMART goals", "specific", "measurable", "achievable", "relevant", "time-bound", "training adherence"], "text": "The SMART goal framework (Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, Time-bound) is widely applied in endurance sport coaching. For marathon training, poorly specified goals such as 'get faster' are less motivationally effective than 'complete the 30 km long run at 5:45/km pace by week 14'. The psychologist node does not modify training prescription but can reinforce goal clarity and motivational rationale in its annotations, explaining why each training block's objectives matter in context of the runner's stated marathon goal."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_goal_setting_intrinsic_motivation_0033", "source_registry_id": "src_goal_setting_self_regulation_marathon_2023", "source_file": "goal_setting_self_regulation_marathon_2023.url", "source_path": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1469029223001401", "source_url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1469029223001401", "source_label": "Self-regulatory processes in goal striving during excellent distance-running performances — ScienceDirect 2023", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["intrinsic motivation", "self-determination theory", "autonomy", "competence", "relatedness", "adherence"], "text": "Self-determination theory (Deci & Ryan) identifies three psychological needs — autonomy, competence, and relatedness — as the foundation of intrinsic motivation in sport. When marathon training goals feel externally imposed or misaligned with the runner's personal values, motivation erodes and burnout risk increases. Psychologist annotations can support intrinsic motivation by connecting training demands to the runner's stated personal meaning: 'This long run represents your commitment to the goal you described — [runner's stated reason for the marathon]'."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_goal_adjustment_mid_race_0034", "source_registry_id": "src_goal_setting_self_regulation_marathon_2023", "source_file": "goal_setting_self_regulation_marathon_2023.url", "source_path": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1469029223001401", "source_url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1469029223001401", "source_label": "Self-regulatory processes in goal striving during excellent distance-running performances — ScienceDirect 2023", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 2, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["goal adjustment", "mid-race", "goal disengagement", "re-engagement", "performance"], "text": "Research on excellent distance-running performances using the Mental Contrasting with Implementation Intentions (MCII) framework found that athletes who periodically contrasted their current state against their goal — and were willing to disengage from an unachievable goal and reengage with a realistic alternative — finished races more successfully than those who rigidly maintained an unrealistic goal. In practice, the psychologist can include race-phase mental check-in prompts ('At km 30, assess and adjust your target if needed') as preparation notes before race day."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_goal_setting_self_concordance_0035", "source_registry_id": "src_goal_setting_sport_exercise_locke_latham_2002", "source_file": "goal_setting_sport_exercise_locke_latham_2002.url", "source_path": "https://med.stanford.edu/content/dam/sm/s-spire/documents/PD.locke-and-latham-retrospective_Paper.pdf", "source_url": "https://med.stanford.edu/content/dam/sm/s-spire/documents/PD.locke-and-latham-retrospective_Paper.pdf", "source_label": "Building a Practically Useful Theory of Goal Setting and Task Motivation — Locke & Latham 2002, American Psychologist", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 3, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["self-concordant goals", "values", "burnout prevention", "well-being", "intrinsic"], "text": "Research by Koestner and Ryan found that individuals pursuing self-concordant goals — goals that align with personal values rather than external pressure — show greater persistence, more positive affect, and lower dropout rates under setbacks. For marathon runners who are training for reasons that deeply matter to them personally, self-concordant framing is protective during difficult training blocks. Psychologist annotations can include brief prompts that reconnect the runner's current week's effort to their stated motivational anchor (race meaning, personal significance)."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_goal_setting_short_long_term_0036", "source_registry_id": "src_goal_setting_sport_exercise_locke_latham_2002", "source_file": "goal_setting_sport_exercise_locke_latham_2002.url", "source_path": "https://med.stanford.edu/content/dam/sm/s-spire/documents/PD.locke-and-latham-retrospective_Paper.pdf", "source_url": "https://med.stanford.edu/content/dam/sm/s-spire/documents/PD.locke-and-latham-retrospective_Paper.pdf", "source_label": "Building a Practically Useful Theory of Goal Setting and Task Motivation — Locke & Latham 2002, American Psychologist", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 3, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["short-term goals", "long-term goals", "milestone", "motivation", "training adherence"], "text": "Long-term goals alone are insufficient to sustain training motivation over a 16–20 week marathon preparation block. Sport psychology research recommends nested goal structures: weekly process goals nested within monthly performance goals nested within the race outcome goal. Reaching each nested milestone provides motivational momentum and self-efficacy reinforcement. The psychologist node can provide milestone recognition scripts — brief affirmations when the runner has completed a key training milestone — as an annotation layer on completed sessions."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_pain_tolerance_endurance_athletes_0037", "source_registry_id": "src_pain_coping_ultra_endurance_pmc_2025", "source_file": "pain_coping_ultra_endurance_pmc_2025.url", "source_path": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12476407/", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12476407/", "source_label": "Human Resilience and Pain Coping Strategies — Elite Ultra-Endurance Athletes Review, PMC 2025", "quality_tier": "systematic_review_meta_analysis", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["pain tolerance", "endurance athletes", "pain threshold", "conditioned pain modulation"], "text": "Meta-analytic research confirms that endurance athletes demonstrate significantly higher pain threshold and tolerance than non-athlete controls, with stronger conditioned pain modulation (CPM) responses. This elevated pain tolerance is partially trainable through both physical training and psychological skill development, particularly cognitive reappraisal. The marathon psychologist node can communicate this evidence to runners experiencing late-training-block physical discomfort, framing muscular fatigue sensations as an expected and normal component of the adaptive process."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_cognitive_reappraisal_pain_0038", "source_registry_id": "src_pain_coping_ultra_endurance_pmc_2025", "source_file": "pain_coping_ultra_endurance_pmc_2025.url", "source_path": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12476407/", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12476407/", "source_label": "Human Resilience and Pain Coping Strategies — Elite Ultra-Endurance Athletes Review, PMC 2025", "quality_tier": "systematic_review_meta_analysis", "page": 2, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["cognitive reappraisal", "pain", "reframing", "meaning", "endurance"], "text": "Cognitive reappraisal — reinterpreting the meaning or implication of a challenging stimulus — is one of the most evidence-supported psychological strategies for managing exercise-induced pain. Ultra-endurance athletes who framed pain as 'part of the reward system' and evidence of productive effort reported higher pain tolerance and performance consistency than those who interpreted pain as a threat signal. Psychologist annotations can include reappraisal scripts: 'The discomfort in your legs at mile 22 is not a warning — it is evidence of your preparation.'"}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_pain_catastrophizing_performance_0039", "source_registry_id": "src_pain_coping_ultra_endurance_pmc_2025", "source_file": "pain_coping_ultra_endurance_pmc_2025.url", "source_path": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12476407/", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12476407/", "source_label": "Human Resilience and Pain Coping Strategies — Elite Ultra-Endurance Athletes Review, PMC 2025", "quality_tier": "systematic_review_meta_analysis", "page": 2, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["pain catastrophizing", "rumination", "anxiety", "performance decrement", "cognitive"], "text": "Pain catastrophizing — a maladaptive cognitive pattern involving magnification of pain, helplessness, and ruminative focus on pain — is among the strongest predictors of reduced pain tolerance and performance impairment in endurance athletes. Runners who catastrophize ('This pain means something is wrong', 'I can't take this anymore') experience pain as more intense and slow down more dramatically than those who use neutral or approach-oriented appraisals. The psychologist node should flag catastrophizing thought patterns and offer reappraisal alternatives when runners have reported high distress in their training log."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_pain_acceptance_vs_suppression_0040", "source_registry_id": "src_mindfulness_distress_tolerance_athlete_2025", "source_file": "mindfulness_distress_tolerance_athlete_2025.url", "source_path": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11939238/", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11939238/", "source_label": "Mindfulness and Athletes' Distress Tolerance: Cognitive Reappraisal and Mental Toughness — PMC 2025", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["pain acceptance", "suppression", "ACT", "mindfulness", "endurance performance"], "text": "Acceptance-based approaches to pain in endurance sport (rooted in ACT and mindfulness traditions) distinguish acceptance — acknowledging pain without judgment and continuing forward — from both suppression (denying pain) and catastrophizing (magnifying it). Research shows that acceptance of discomfort is associated with better endurance performance and lower post-race distress than suppression strategies. The psychologist node can include acceptance-oriented pain scripts: 'Your legs are telling you this is hard. It is hard. Keep moving.'"}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_pain_experience_wall_mile20_0041", "source_registry_id": "src_marathon_hitting_wall_mental_strategies_2026", "source_file": "marathon_hitting_wall_mental_strategies_2026.url", "source_path": "https://runlovers.it/en/2026/marathon-mental-strategies-hitting-the-wall/", "source_url": "https://runlovers.it/en/2026/marathon-mental-strategies-hitting-the-wall/", "source_label": "Marathon Mindset: Proven Strategies to Survive the Wall and Cross the Finish Line — Runlovers 2026", "quality_tier": "practitioner_review", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["pain", "hitting the wall", "mile 20", "glycogen", "perception of effort", "Marcora"], "text": "Professor Samuele Marcora's research at Kent University demonstrates that when marathoners slow or stop, residual muscular glycogen remains — the decision is generated by the brain's perception of effort rather than muscular failure. This psychophysiological finding reframes the experience of hitting the wall: the barrier is primarily cognitive and manageable, not purely physiological. Understanding this mechanism gives runners evidence-based reason to trust that forward movement remains possible even when perception says otherwise."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_pain_distraction_methods_0042", "source_registry_id": "src_marathon_hitting_wall_mental_strategies_2026", "source_file": "marathon_hitting_wall_mental_strategies_2026.url", "source_path": "https://runlovers.it/en/2026/marathon-mental-strategies-hitting-the-wall/", "source_url": "https://runlovers.it/en/2026/marathon-mental-strategies-hitting-the-wall/", "source_label": "Marathon Mindset: Proven Strategies to Survive the Wall and Cross the Finish Line — Runlovers 2026", "quality_tier": "practitioner_review", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["dissociation", "distraction", "pain management", "crowd", "external focus", "mental arithmetic"], "text": "Research-supported distraction techniques for pain management during a marathon include: (1) external focus on crowd energy or scenery, (2) mental arithmetic (calculating splits, counting markers), (3) positive dissociation — running for a specific person or cause rather than oneself. Dissociative distraction is most effective for non-elite runners and correlates with later onset of severe fatigue perception. The psychologist node can include race-phase distraction options as part of race-day preparation notes, particularly for the challenging 25–35 km segment."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_pain_mindfulness_tolerance_2025_0043", "source_registry_id": "src_mindfulness_distress_tolerance_athlete_2025", "source_file": "mindfulness_distress_tolerance_athlete_2025.url", "source_path": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11939238/", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11939238/", "source_label": "Mindfulness and Athletes' Distress Tolerance: Cognitive Reappraisal and Mental Toughness — PMC 2025", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["mindfulness", "pain tolerance", "distress tolerance", "mental toughness", "cognitive reappraisal"], "text": "A 2025 PMC study found significant positive correlations between mindfulness, cognitive reappraisal, and mental toughness in athletes, with cognitive reappraisal mediating the relationship between mindfulness and distress tolerance. Athletes higher in mindfulness were better able to reappraise painful sensations as non-threatening, sustaining effort more effectively. Regular mindfulness practice during training — even brief 5-minute body scan sessions — appears sufficient to produce measurable improvements in pain tolerance over a training block."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_pain_self_efficacy_link_0044", "source_registry_id": "src_pain_coping_ultra_endurance_pmc_2025", "source_file": "pain_coping_ultra_endurance_pmc_2025.url", "source_path": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12476407/", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12476407/", "source_label": "Human Resilience and Pain Coping Strategies — Elite Ultra-Endurance Athletes Review, PMC 2025", "quality_tier": "systematic_review_meta_analysis", "page": 3, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["self-efficacy", "pain self-efficacy", "confidence", "tolerance", "injury recovery"], "text": "Pain self-efficacy — a runner's belief in their ability to manage or cope with pain — is a distinct predictor of pain tolerance beyond baseline fitness or pain threshold. High pain self-efficacy correlates with faster return-to-sport after injury, higher completion rates in difficult training sessions, and reduced pain interference with performance. The psychologist node can support pain self-efficacy through confidence-building scripts referencing the runner's own training history: 'You have tolerated comparable discomfort in previous long runs and continued — this is evidence of your capacity.'"}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_prerace_routine_theory_0045", "source_registry_id": "src_prerace_routine_psychological_effects_2025", "source_file": "prerace_routine_psychological_effects_2025.url", "source_path": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/395585347_Pre-Game_Routines_and_Their_Psychological_Effects_on_Performance_Consistency", "source_url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/395585347_Pre-Game_Routines_and_Their_Psychological_Effects_on_Performance_Consistency", "source_label": "Pre-Game Routines and Their Psychological Effects on Performance Consistency — ResearchGate 2025", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["pre-race routine", "psychological preparation", "arousal regulation", "consistency", "confidence"], "text": "Pre-performance routines are among the best-supported psychological tools in sport psychology, with consistent evidence across team sports, individual sports, and endurance contexts. Routines provide two key benefits: (1) they automate the pre-competition period, preserving cognitive resources for performance; (2) they allow the athlete to regulate arousal to their individual optimal zone. For marathon runners, a pre-race routine spanning the final 48 hours through the starting corral is a psychologically coherent preparation structure."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_prerace_arousal_zone_0046", "source_registry_id": "src_prerace_routine_psychological_effects_2025", "source_file": "prerace_routine_psychological_effects_2025.url", "source_path": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/395585347_Pre-Game_Routines_and_Their_Psychological_Effects_on_Performance_Consistency", "source_url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/395585347_Pre-Game_Routines_and_Their_Psychological_Effects_on_Performance_Consistency", "source_label": "Pre-Game Routines and Their Psychological Effects on Performance Consistency — ResearchGate 2025", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["arousal", "optimal zone", "individual zone of optimal functioning", "IZOF", "anxiety", "performance"], "text": "Hanin's Individual Zone of Optimal Functioning (IZOF) model establishes that each athlete has a personal optimal arousal range for peak performance — not a universal level but an individually calibrated state. Pre-race anxiety below this zone leads to flat, underperforming execution; anxiety above the zone leads to tight, error-prone running. Pre-race routines should be designed to bring the runner's arousal level into their personal optimal zone by race start. The psychologist node can include a brief arousal self-check prompt in race-week preparation notes: 'On a scale of 1–10, where do you feel most focused and ready — that is your target on race morning.'"}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_prerace_controllables_0047", "source_registry_id": "src_prerace_anxiety_runners_guide_2025", "source_file": "prerace_anxiety_runners_guide_2025.url", "source_path": "https://runnersconnect.net/beat-pre-race-anxiety-the-runners-guide-to-managing-performance-nerves-2025/", "source_url": "https://runnersconnect.net/beat-pre-race-anxiety-the-runners-guide-to-managing-performance-nerves-2025/", "source_label": "Beat Pre Race Anxiety: The Runner's Guide to Managing Performance Nerves — Runners Connect 2025", "quality_tier": "practitioner_review", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["controllable", "uncontrollable", "pre-race anxiety", "control focus", "race preparation"], "text": "A major driver of pre-race anxiety is allocation of attention to uncontrollable race variables: weather, course conditions, competitor performance. Sport psychology consistently recommends a 'control the controllables' intervention: systematically redirecting planning and attention toward things entirely within the runner's control — kit preparation, sleep routine, nutrition timing, warm-up sequence, and self-talk readiness. The psychologist node can include a pre-race 'controllables checklist' as a race-week annotation, providing a structured focus redirect that reduces anxiety arousal."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_prerace_self_efficacy_building_0048", "source_registry_id": "src_prerace_anxiety_runners_guide_2025", "source_file": "prerace_anxiety_runners_guide_2025.url", "source_path": "https://runnersconnect.net/beat-pre-race-anxiety-the-runners-guide-to-managing-performance-nerves-2025/", "source_url": "https://runnersconnect.net/beat-pre-race-anxiety-the-runners-guide-to-managing-performance-nerves-2025/", "source_label": "Beat Pre Race Anxiety: The Runner's Guide to Managing Performance Nerves — Runners Connect 2025", "quality_tier": "practitioner_review", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["self-efficacy", "confidence", "evidence review", "training history", "pre-race"], "text": "Low self-efficacy — underestimating one's capacity to handle the race — is a primary predictor of pre-race anxiety and underperformance. Bandura's self-efficacy theory identifies four sources: past performance accomplishments, vicarious experience, verbal persuasion, and physiological interpretation. Pre-race confidence is most powerfully built through a structured review of past performance accomplishments: revisiting completed training sessions, key long runs, and prior race splits provides concrete evidence of readiness. The psychologist node should generate a 'training evidence summary' in the race-week annotation, citing the runner's actual training data."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_prerace_cognitive_restructuring_0049", "source_registry_id": "src_prerace_anxiety_runners_guide_2025", "source_file": "prerace_anxiety_runners_guide_2025.url", "source_path": "https://runnersconnect.net/beat-pre-race-anxiety-the-runners-guide-to-managing-performance-nerves-2025/", "source_url": "https://runnersconnect.net/beat-pre-race-anxiety-the-runners-guide-to-managing-performance-nerves-2025/", "source_label": "Beat Pre Race Anxiety: The Runner's Guide to Managing Performance Nerves — Runners Connect 2025", "quality_tier": "practitioner_review", "page": 2, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["cognitive restructuring", "CBT", "pre-race", "anxiety", "worry", "evidence-based"], "text": "Cognitive-behavioral approaches to pre-race anxiety involve identifying specific worry thoughts and examining the evidence for and against them — rather than suppressing the thoughts or accepting them uncritically. A systematic review by Bühlmayer et al. confirmed that cognitive restructuring techniques significantly reduced competitive anxiety in endurance athletes. Common pre-race cognitive restructuring example: worry thought — 'I haven't trained enough'; reframe — 'I completed 14 of 16 scheduled long runs and all interval sessions; my preparation is solid'."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_prerace_breathing_relaxation_0050", "source_registry_id": "src_prerace_routine_psychological_effects_2025", "source_file": "prerace_routine_psychological_effects_2025.url", "source_path": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/395585347_Pre-Game_Routines_and_Their_Psychological_Effects_on_Performance_Consistency", "source_url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/395585347_Pre-Game_Routines_and_Their_Psychological_Effects_on_Performance_Consistency", "source_label": "Pre-Game Routines and Their Psychological Effects on Performance Consistency — ResearchGate 2025", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 2, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["breathing", "box breathing", "arousal regulation", "activation", "pre-race calm"], "text": "Controlled breathing techniques are among the most accessible and evidence-based tools for arousal regulation before a marathon. Box breathing (inhale 4 counts, hold 4, exhale 4, hold 4) activates the parasympathetic nervous system within 2–3 minutes, reliably reducing heart rate and subjective anxiety. Activation breathing (short, sharp inhales, forceful exhales) can raise arousal from an under-activated state. The psychologist node can provide breathing protocol scripts matched to the runner's pre-race arousal pattern: calming protocol for high-anxiety runners, activating protocol for flat or under-motivated runners."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_prerace_ritual_vs_superstition_0051", "source_registry_id": "src_prerace_routine_psychological_effects_2025", "source_file": "prerace_routine_psychological_effects_2025.url", "source_path": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/395585347_Pre-Game_Routines_and_Their_Psychological_Effects_on_Performance_Consistency", "source_url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/395585347_Pre-Game_Routines_and_Their_Psychological_Effects_on_Performance_Consistency", "source_label": "Pre-Game Routines and Their Psychological Effects on Performance Consistency — ResearchGate 2025", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 2, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["ritual", "superstition", "pre-performance", "automaticity", "psychological control"], "text": "Sport psychology distinguishes strategic pre-race rituals from performance-impeding superstition. A strategic ritual is explainable and replicable: 'I do box breathing because it regulates my arousal state.' A superstition is arbitrary: 'I must wear my left sock first or the race will go badly.' Rituals work because they create controllable, predictable sequences that signal psychological readiness; superstitions create fragility and anxiety when the arbitrary condition cannot be met. Psychologist annotations should encourage runners to build transparent, rationale-driven routines."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_prerace_sleep_mental_0052", "source_registry_id": "src_prerace_anxiety_runners_guide_2025", "source_file": "prerace_anxiety_runners_guide_2025.url", "source_path": "https://runnersconnect.net/beat-pre-race-anxiety-the-runners-guide-to-managing-performance-nerves-2025/", "source_url": "https://runnersconnect.net/beat-pre-race-anxiety-the-runners-guide-to-managing-performance-nerves-2025/", "source_label": "Beat Pre Race Anxiety: The Runner's Guide to Managing Performance Nerves — Runners Connect 2025", "quality_tier": "practitioner_review", "page": 2, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["sleep", "pre-race night", "anxiety", "insomnia", "race performance", "sleep deprivation"], "text": "Pre-race night insomnia is extremely common in marathon runners and is not predictive of performance impairment when the preceding training block included adequate sleep. Research shows that a single night of poor sleep before a race has minimal impact on endurance performance, provided the athlete has been well-rested during the training taper. Psychologist notes for race week should proactively include this evidence to reduce anxiety about sleep: knowing that one poor sleep night will not significantly harm performance breaks the anxiety-insomnia spiral."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_wall_definition_epidemiology_0053", "source_registry_id": "src_marathon_hitting_wall_mental_strategies_2026", "source_file": "marathon_hitting_wall_mental_strategies_2026.url", "source_path": "https://runlovers.it/en/2026/marathon-mental-strategies-hitting-the-wall/", "source_url": "https://runlovers.it/en/2026/marathon-mental-strategies-hitting-the-wall/", "source_label": "Marathon Mindset: Proven Strategies to Survive the Wall and Cross the Finish Line — Runlovers 2026", "quality_tier": "practitioner_review", "page": 2, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["hitting the wall", "bonk", "marathon", "glycogen depletion", "prevalence"], "text": "Research indicates that 43–53% of marathon runners experience hitting the wall — characterized by four features: generalized fatigue beyond expected tiredness, unintentional pace reduction, strong desire to walk, and a psychological shift from performance focus to pure survival. Understanding the prevalence and phenomenology of the wall normalizes the experience for runners and supports a proactive preparation mindset. The psychologist node should include a brief description of the wall in race-week notes for runners completing their first or second marathon."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_wall_brain_effort_perception_0054", "source_registry_id": "src_marathon_hitting_wall_mental_strategies_2026", "source_file": "marathon_hitting_wall_mental_strategies_2026.url", "source_path": "https://runlovers.it/en/2026/marathon-mental-strategies-hitting-the-wall/", "source_url": "https://runlovers.it/en/2026/marathon-mental-strategies-hitting-the-wall/", "source_label": "Marathon Mindset: Proven Strategies to Survive the Wall and Cross the Finish Line — Runlovers 2026", "quality_tier": "practitioner_review", "page": 2, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["perception of effort", "Marcora", "brain", "central governor", "effort", "psychobiological model"], "text": "Marcora's psychobiological model of endurance establishes that the decision to slow or stop is generated by the brain's perception of effort relative to its willingness to continue, not by muscular failure per se. This model predicts that psychological interventions — self-talk, distraction, reappraisal — can alter the brain's effort perception independently of physiological state. The implication for marathon runners: building a mental skills toolkit for the wall is a legitimate performance investment, not a psychological luxury."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_wall_acceptance_strategy_0055", "source_registry_id": "src_marathon_hitting_wall_mental_strategies_2026", "source_file": "marathon_hitting_wall_mental_strategies_2026.url", "source_path": "https://runlovers.it/en/2026/marathon-mental-strategies-hitting-the-wall/", "source_url": "https://runlovers.it/en/2026/marathon-mental-strategies-hitting-the-wall/", "source_label": "Marathon Mindset: Proven Strategies to Survive the Wall and Cross the Finish Line — Runlovers 2026", "quality_tier": "practitioner_review", "page": 3, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["acceptance", "wall", "discomfort", "psychological preparation", "pain normalization"], "text": "Acceptance is the foundational psychological strategy for managing the wall: beginning the race with the mental preparation that difficulty, fatigue, and psychological resistance will occur in the final third. Runners who start a marathon already having accepted that the last 10 km will be hard are better positioned cognitively than those who expect the race to feel consistently manageable. The psychologist node can include a 'race acceptance script' in final preparation notes: 'The last 10 km will be the hardest. That is the race. You are prepared for it.'"}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_wall_chunking_strategy_0056", "source_registry_id": "src_marathon_hitting_wall_mental_strategies_2026", "source_file": "marathon_hitting_wall_mental_strategies_2026.url", "source_path": "https://runlovers.it/en/2026/marathon-mental-strategies-hitting-the-wall/", "source_url": "https://runlovers.it/en/2026/marathon-mental-strategies-hitting-the-wall/", "source_label": "Marathon Mindset: Proven Strategies to Survive the Wall and Cross the Finish Line — Runlovers 2026", "quality_tier": "practitioner_review", "page": 3, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["chunking", "segmentation", "distance management", "mental units", "cognitive load"], "text": "Chunking — mentally dividing the remaining race distance into small, manageable units — is a well-supported strategy for managing the wall. When 6 miles feel impossible as a whole, 'run to the next aid station' is psychologically achievable. Research on endurance psychology confirms that chunking reduces the perceived cognitive burden of remaining distance and increases runners' sense of control. Practical chunk sizes: aid stations (~1.5 miles), kilometer markers, or time-based units (the next 10 minutes)."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_wall_goal_adjustment_0057", "source_registry_id": "src_marathon_hitting_wall_mental_strategies_2026", "source_file": "marathon_hitting_wall_mental_strategies_2026.url", "source_path": "https://runlovers.it/en/2026/marathon-mental-strategies-hitting-the-wall/", "source_url": "https://runlovers.it/en/2026/marathon-mental-strategies-hitting-the-wall/", "source_label": "Marathon Mindset: Proven Strategies to Survive the Wall and Cross the Finish Line — Runlovers 2026", "quality_tier": "practitioner_review", "page": 4, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["goal adjustment", "B goal", "completion", "wall", "flexibility", "cognitive flexibility"], "text": "At the wall, rigid adherence to an A-goal time target when physiological and psychological resources are critically depleted produces the worst outcomes. Sport psychology recommends pre-committing to a goal adjustment protocol: at a defined struggle point, the runner explicitly releases the time goal and adopts a completion or execution goal. Research shows that this deliberate goal pivot — rather than gradual drift into despair — preserves more motivational energy and produces better finish times than clinging to an unachievable target. This protocol should be briefed explicitly in race-week psychologist notes."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_wall_social_motivation_0058", "source_registry_id": "src_marathon_hitting_wall_mental_strategies_2026", "source_file": "marathon_hitting_wall_mental_strategies_2026.url", "source_path": "https://runlovers.it/en/2026/marathon-mental-strategies-hitting-the-wall/", "source_url": "https://runlovers.it/en/2026/marathon-mental-strategies-hitting-the-wall/", "source_label": "Marathon Mindset: Proven Strategies to Survive the Wall and Cross the Finish Line — Runlovers 2026", "quality_tier": "practitioner_review", "page": 4, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["social motivation", "running for others", "positive dissociation", "charity", "motivation anchor"], "text": "When intrinsic motivation collapses at the wall, redirecting motivation to an external anchor — running for a specific person, cause, or commitment — provides a powerful alternative motivational source. This technique, sometimes called positive dissociation, shifts the emotional reference point from self-centered pain to outward-facing purpose. Runners who have identified a specific person they are running for before the race report greater resilience at the wall. The psychologist node can invite runners to identify their motivation anchor in the race preparation notes phase."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_wall_cognitive_engagement_0059", "source_registry_id": "src_marathon_hitting_wall_mental_strategies_2026", "source_file": "marathon_hitting_wall_mental_strategies_2026.url", "source_path": "https://runlovers.it/en/2026/marathon-mental-strategies-hitting-the-wall/", "source_url": "https://runlovers.it/en/2026/marathon-mental-strategies-hitting-the-wall/", "source_label": "Marathon Mindset: Proven Strategies to Survive the Wall and Cross the Finish Line — Runlovers 2026", "quality_tier": "practitioner_review", "page": 5, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["cognitive engagement", "decision-making", "active running", "wall", "mental dropout"], "text": "Research on wall experiences shows that passive cognitive dropout — allowing attention to spiral into pain catastrophizing or despairing thoughts — significantly worsens both the subjective experience and the objective pace outcome. Staying cognitively active at the wall — counting steps, actively choosing a focus strategy, engaging with the environment — prevents the passive negative spiral. The psychologist node can include a 'stay cognitively active at mile 20' script as part of race-phase mental preparation notes."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_wall_mantra_preparation_0060", "source_registry_id": "src_marathon_hitting_wall_mental_strategies_2026", "source_file": "marathon_hitting_wall_mental_strategies_2026.url", "source_path": "https://runlovers.it/en/2026/marathon-mental-strategies-hitting-the-wall/", "source_url": "https://runlovers.it/en/2026/marathon-mental-strategies-hitting-the-wall/", "source_label": "Marathon Mindset: Proven Strategies to Survive the Wall and Cross the Finish Line — Runlovers 2026", "quality_tier": "practitioner_review", "page": 5, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["mantra", "wall", "crisis point", "pre-planned", "self-talk preparation"], "text": "Runners who have pre-planned specific mantras for their anticipated wall moment — rather than improvising self-talk under stress — report greater confidence and more effective forward momentum during race crises. A wall-specific mantra should be: brief, honest (not denying difficulty), approach-oriented, and personally meaningful. Examples: 'This is the race', 'You know this feeling', 'One more minute'. The psychologist node should include wall-specific mantra suggestions in race-phase preparation notes, distinct from general training mantras."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_mindfulness_mbsr_sport_0061", "source_registry_id": "src_mindfulness_sport_mbi_umbrella_2025", "source_file": "mindfulness_sport_mbi_umbrella_2025.url", "source_path": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1728869X2500053X", "source_url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1728869X2500053X", "source_label": "Impact of mindfulness-based interventions on sports performance and mental health: An umbrella review — ScienceDirect 2025", "quality_tier": "systematic_review_meta_analysis", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["mindfulness", "MBSR", "sport performance", "stress reduction", "mental health"], "text": "Mindfulness-Based Stress Reduction (MBSR) and sport-adapted mindfulness protocols have accumulated strong evidence for reducing psychological distress, competitive anxiety, and burnout in endurance athletes. An umbrella review (ScienceDirect 2025) confirms MBIs reliably lower cognitive and somatic anxiety and improve emotional resilience in distance runners. For marathon preparation, even brief daily mindfulness practice (5–10 minutes) during the training block produces measurable improvements in stress regulation by race week."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_mindfulness_present_moment_0062", "source_registry_id": "src_mindfulness_sport_mbi_umbrella_2025", "source_file": "mindfulness_sport_mbi_umbrella_2025.url", "source_path": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1728869X2500053X", "source_url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1728869X2500053X", "source_label": "Impact of mindfulness-based interventions on sports performance and mental health: An umbrella review — ScienceDirect 2025", "quality_tier": "systematic_review_meta_analysis", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["mindfulness", "present moment", "rumination", "worry", "race focus"], "text": "Mindfulness training's core mechanism in sport is the development of present-moment focus: the ability to notice thoughts and sensations without being pulled into rumination about past performances or anxiety about future outcomes. In a marathon race context, present-moment awareness prevents the 'kilometer 20 catastrophe loop' — the recursive worry about remaining distance and pace viability. Psychologist annotations can include a brief mindfulness prompt for each race phase: 'Right now, notice only this kilometer. What is your body doing well?'"}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_mac_protocol_overview_0063", "source_registry_id": "src_act_mac_sport_gardner_moore", "source_file": "act_mac_sport_gardner_moore.url", "source_path": "https://dreddieoconnor.com/blog/acceptance-and-commitment-therapy/", "source_url": "https://dreddieoconnor.com/blog/acceptance-and-commitment-therapy/", "source_label": "Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) in Sport — Dr. Eddie O'Connor Sports Psychologist review", "quality_tier": "expert_review", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["MAC", "Mindfulness Acceptance Commitment", "Gardner", "Moore", "sport psychology protocol"], "text": "The Mindfulness-Acceptance-Commitment (MAC) protocol, developed by Gardner and Moore (2007), is the most empirically validated mindfulness-based intervention designed specifically for athletes. MAC integrates ACT principles — psychological flexibility, values clarification, committed action — with mindfulness training. It produces performance benefits through increasing the athlete's ability to stay present and engaged with valued action even while experiencing difficult thoughts and emotions. Case studies show marked performance improvements in elite athletes following 8-session MAC protocols."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_act_psychological_flexibility_0064", "source_registry_id": "src_act_mac_sport_gardner_moore", "source_file": "act_mac_sport_gardner_moore.url", "source_path": "https://dreddieoconnor.com/blog/acceptance-and-commitment-therapy/", "source_url": "https://dreddieoconnor.com/blog/acceptance-and-commitment-therapy/", "source_label": "Acceptance and Commitment Therapy (ACT) in Sport — Dr. Eddie O'Connor Sports Psychologist review", "quality_tier": "expert_review", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["ACT", "psychological flexibility", "defusion", "values", "committed action", "endurance"], "text": "ACT's key construct for endurance athletes is psychological flexibility: the ability to stay in contact with the present moment, open to difficult internal experiences, and committed to values-driven action regardless of uncomfortable thoughts or sensations. In a marathon context, psychological flexibility means continuing to run at target pace even when unhelpful thoughts arise ('I'm falling apart') — observing the thought without being controlled by it. This defusion from unhelpful thoughts is a trainable skill with direct performance implications."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_mindfulness_attention_regulation_0065", "source_registry_id": "src_mindfulness_sport_mbi_umbrella_2025", "source_file": "mindfulness_sport_mbi_umbrella_2025.url", "source_path": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1728869X2500053X", "source_url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1728869X2500053X", "source_label": "Impact of mindfulness-based interventions on sports performance and mental health: An umbrella review — ScienceDirect 2025", "quality_tier": "systematic_review_meta_analysis", "page": 2, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["mindfulness", "attention regulation", "focus", "distraction", "performance"], "text": "Mindfulness training improves attention regulation — the ability to intentionally direct and sustain focus, and to notice when attention has wandered without judgment. For marathon runners, this translates directly to improved pacing accuracy (maintaining intended attention to effort rather than drifting into distraction-induced pace errors) and better recovery from adverse events during a race (refocusing after a fall or a missed split). Research from BMC Sports Science (2024) confirms a strong association between mindfulness and athletic mental skills, mediated by emotion regulation."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_mindfulness_pre_session_routine_0066", "source_registry_id": "src_mindfulness_sport_mbi_umbrella_2025", "source_file": "mindfulness_sport_mbi_umbrella_2025.url", "source_path": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1728869X2500053X", "source_url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1728869X2500053X", "source_label": "Impact of mindfulness-based interventions on sports performance and mental health: An umbrella review — ScienceDirect 2025", "quality_tier": "systematic_review_meta_analysis", "page": 2, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["mindfulness", "pre-training", "body scan", "centering", "routine"], "text": "A 2–5 minute mindfulness body scan before key training sessions — noting physical sensations without judgment, releasing unnecessary tension, and setting an intentional focus for the session — supports both training quality and mental skill development. Research suggests this pre-session centering routine gradually reduces the training mental barrier and increases the athlete's sense of purposeful engagement with each workout. The psychologist node can include session-specific centering prompts as an annotation layer on high-intensity or long-run sessions."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_mindfulness_post_session_reflection_0067", "source_registry_id": "src_mindfulness_sport_mbi_umbrella_2025", "source_file": "mindfulness_sport_mbi_umbrella_2025.url", "source_path": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1728869X2500053X", "source_url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1728869X2500053X", "source_label": "Impact of mindfulness-based interventions on sports performance and mental health: An umbrella review — ScienceDirect 2025", "quality_tier": "systematic_review_meta_analysis", "page": 3, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["mindfulness", "post-run", "reflection", "self-compassion", "growth mindset"], "text": "Post-session mindful reflection — briefly noting what went well, what was challenging, and what was learned, without evaluative self-judgment — supports both psychological recovery and skill development. Self-compassion, a component of mindfulness practice, predicts resilience after poor performances and prevents the negative rumination spiral that can impair motivation after a difficult session. Psychologist annotations for completed sessions can include a brief structured reflection prompt, particularly after sessions the runner has flagged as difficult."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_mindfulness_race_anxiety_rct_0068", "source_registry_id": "src_mindfulness_sport_mbi_umbrella_2025", "source_file": "mindfulness_sport_mbi_umbrella_2025.url", "source_path": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1728869X2500053X", "source_url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1728869X2500053X", "source_label": "Impact of mindfulness-based interventions on sports performance and mental health: An umbrella review — ScienceDirect 2025", "quality_tier": "systematic_review_meta_analysis", "page": 3, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["mindfulness", "race anxiety", "randomized controlled trial", "distance runners", "intervention"], "text": "A randomized controlled trial in the MBI umbrella review (ScienceDirect 2025) specifically reported a significant decline in race-related anxiety for distance runners following an 8-week mindfulness-based intervention. The anxiety reduction was associated with improved pre-race self-efficacy and more consistent race execution. This evidence supports incorporating brief mindfulness practices throughout the marathon training plan rather than only in race week — the benefits accumulate over weeks of consistent practice."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_overtraining_burnout_definition_0069", "source_registry_id": "src_motivation_overtraining_burnout_sdt_2007", "source_file": "motivation_overtraining_burnout_sdt_2007.url", "source_path": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17461390701302607", "source_url": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17461390701302607", "source_label": "Motivation, overtraining, and burnout: Can self-determined motivation predict overtraining and burnout in elite athletes? — Taylor & Francis 2007", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["overtraining", "burnout", "psychology", "fatigue", "motivation loss"], "text": "Burnout in sport is defined as a psychological, physical, and emotional withdrawal from a formerly enjoyable and motivating activity resulting from prolonged or chronic stress. It is distinct from acute overtraining fatigue: overtraining is physiological and resolves with adequate rest, while burnout involves psychological disengagement that requires motivational and values-based recovery. The psychologist node should distinguish these two states in its explanatory annotations and recommend professional support for burnout presentations."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_overtraining_sdt_motivation_0070", "source_registry_id": "src_motivation_overtraining_burnout_sdt_2007", "source_file": "motivation_overtraining_burnout_sdt_2007.url", "source_path": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17461390701302607", "source_url": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17461390701302607", "source_label": "Motivation, overtraining, and burnout: Can self-determined motivation predict overtraining and burnout in elite athletes? — Taylor & Francis 2007", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 1, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["SDT", "self-determination theory", "autonomy", "overtraining", "burnout prediction"], "text": "Research (Lemyre et al., 2007 — Taylor & Francis) confirms that athletes with lower self-determined motivation are significantly more likely to develop overtraining syndrome and burnout. SDT predicts that when autonomy, competence, and relatedness needs are unmet — for example, when training feels externally controlled or disconnected from personal values — motivation erodes under training load. The psychologist node can include brief autonomy-support notes in high-load weeks: explaining the rationale for a demanding session reconnects it to the runner's stated goals and restores the autonomy-of-purpose that sustains motivation."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_overtraining_warning_signs_0071", "source_registry_id": "src_motivation_overtraining_burnout_sdt_2007", "source_file": "motivation_overtraining_burnout_sdt_2007.url", "source_path": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17461390701302607", "source_url": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17461390701302607", "source_label": "Motivation, overtraining, and burnout: Can self-determined motivation predict overtraining and burnout in elite athletes? — Taylor & Francis 2007", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 2, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["overtraining warning signs", "mood disturbance", "POMS", "motivation loss", "irritability"], "text": "Early psychological warning signs of overtraining syndrome overlap substantially with depression: persistent low motivation, irritability, sleep disturbance, elevated perceived exertion at submaximal efforts, and loss of enjoyment in previously motivating activities. The Profile of Mood States (POMS) measure detects early overtraining indicators via mood shifts, particularly vigor reduction and fatigue increase, before physiological markers become prominent. The psychologist node should flag combinations of these mood indicators in runner self-reports, recommending coach review before advancing training load."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_overtraining_prevention_load_0072", "source_registry_id": "src_motivation_overtraining_burnout_sdt_2007", "source_file": "motivation_overtraining_burnout_sdt_2007.url", "source_path": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17461390701302607", "source_url": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17461390701302607", "source_label": "Motivation, overtraining, and burnout: Can self-determined motivation predict overtraining and burnout in elite athletes? — Taylor & Francis 2007", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 2, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["overtraining prevention", "recovery", "periodization", "load management", "mental health"], "text": "Prevention of overtraining syndrome requires both physical periodization (planned recovery weeks and deload phases) and psychological monitoring. Research shows that cutting back on training load and intensity when early warning signs appear is associated with rapid recovery of positive mood states and motivation. The psychologist node should not prescribe load changes — that is the executor's role — but can provide evidence-based explanations for why scheduled recovery weeks serve both physiological and psychological protective functions."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_motivation_intrinsic_adherence_0073", "source_registry_id": "src_motivation_overtraining_burnout_sdt_2007", "source_file": "motivation_overtraining_burnout_sdt_2007.url", "source_path": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17461390701302607", "source_url": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17461390701302607", "source_label": "Motivation, overtraining, and burnout: Can self-determined motivation predict overtraining and burnout in elite athletes? — Taylor & Francis 2007", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 3, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["intrinsic motivation", "adherence", "long-term", "enjoyment", "values", "meaning"], "text": "Long-term training adherence in amateur marathon runners is primarily predicted by intrinsic motivation — running because it is personally meaningful, enjoyable, or identity-aligned — rather than extrinsic motivation (performance targets, social approval). Research consistently shows that intrinsic motivators sustain training through difficult periods while extrinsic motivators lose effectiveness under fatigue or setbacks. The psychologist node can support intrinsic motivation by periodically reconnecting training demands to the runner's stated personal meaning and values in its annotations."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_motivation_fun_and_variety_0074", "source_registry_id": "src_motivation_overtraining_burnout_sdt_2007", "source_file": "motivation_overtraining_burnout_sdt_2007.url", "source_path": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17461390701302607", "source_url": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17461390701302607", "source_label": "Motivation, overtraining, and burnout: Can self-determined motivation predict overtraining and burnout in elite athletes? — Taylor & Francis 2007", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 3, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["variety", "enjoyment", "training motivation", "monotony", "renewal", "adherence"], "text": "Sport psychology recommendations for preventing motivational burnout during long training blocks include incorporating variety — varying route, terrain, training partner, workout format — to maintain novelty and enjoyment. Monotony is an independent predictor of dropout and motivation loss even when physiological load remains appropriate. The psychologist node can suggest experiential variety as an annotation in multi-week training phases, particularly during mid-block phases when motivation characteristically dips."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_motivation_social_support_0075", "source_registry_id": "src_motivation_overtraining_burnout_sdt_2007", "source_file": "motivation_overtraining_burnout_sdt_2007.url", "source_path": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17461390701302607", "source_url": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17461390701302607", "source_label": "Motivation, overtraining, and burnout: Can self-determined motivation predict overtraining and burnout in elite athletes? — Taylor & Francis 2007", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 4, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["social support", "accountability", "community", "training partner", "motivation"], "text": "Social support is one of the strongest predictors of long-term exercise adherence in recreational athletes. Training with a partner or group increases accountability, provides relatedness need-satisfaction (per SDT), and buffers stress. Research confirms that joining a running group or having a virtual accountability partner through tracking apps significantly increases training completion rates. Psychologist annotations can acknowledge social training context in the runner's profile and include brief suggestions for community-based motivation strategies during solitary high-load blocks."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_motivation_milestone_recognition_0076", "source_registry_id": "src_goal_setting_sport_exercise_locke_latham_2002", "source_file": "goal_setting_sport_exercise_locke_latham_2002.url", "source_path": "https://med.stanford.edu/content/dam/sm/s-spire/documents/PD.locke-and-latham-retrospective_Paper.pdf", "source_url": "https://med.stanford.edu/content/dam/sm/s-spire/documents/PD.locke-and-latham-retrospective_Paper.pdf", "source_label": "Building a Practically Useful Theory of Goal Setting and Task Motivation — Locke & Latham 2002, American Psychologist", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 4, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["milestone", "recognition", "progress tracking", "self-efficacy", "motivation"], "text": "Regular recognition of training milestones — completing a long run PB, finishing the first back-to-back long run weekend, completing 6 consecutive weeks — provides self-efficacy reinforcement and motivational momentum. Research on goal-setting and motivation confirms that acknowledging progress toward intermediate milestones is critical for sustaining effort toward long-term goals. The psychologist node can generate brief milestone recognition annotations when runners complete significant training achievements, framing the accomplishment in terms of race readiness."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_anxiety_IZOF_optimal_0077", "source_registry_id": "src_prerace_anxiety_runners_guide_2025", "source_file": "prerace_anxiety_runners_guide_2025.url", "source_path": "https://runnersconnect.net/beat-pre-race-anxiety-the-runners-guide-to-managing-performance-nerves-2025/", "source_url": "https://runnersconnect.net/beat-pre-race-anxiety-the-runners-guide-to-managing-performance-nerves-2025/", "source_label": "Beat Pre Race Anxiety: The Runner's Guide to Managing Performance Nerves — Runners Connect 2025", "quality_tier": "practitioner_review", "page": 3, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["IZOF", "optimal anxiety", "performance", "individual zone", "facilitating anxiety"], "text": "Hanin's Individual Zone of Optimal Functioning (IZOF) model establishes that some anxiety is beneficial for marathon performance — it is the level and quality of anxiety that matters, not its presence. Anxiety within the individual's optimal zone facilitates performance; anxiety outside it (too high or too low) impairs it. A critical message for anxious runners: the goal is not to eliminate pre-race nerves but to understand them as a performance preparation signal, and to manage their intensity toward the optimal zone."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_anxiety_cognitive_vs_somatic_0078", "source_registry_id": "src_prerace_anxiety_runners_guide_2025", "source_file": "prerace_anxiety_runners_guide_2025.url", "source_path": "https://runnersconnect.net/beat-pre-race-anxiety-the-runners-guide-to-managing-performance-nerves-2025/", "source_url": "https://runnersconnect.net/beat-pre-race-anxiety-the-runners-guide-to-managing-performance-nerves-2025/", "source_label": "Beat Pre Race Anxiety: The Runner's Guide to Managing Performance Nerves — Runners Connect 2025", "quality_tier": "practitioner_review", "page": 3, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["cognitive anxiety", "somatic anxiety", "pre-race", "worry", "physical symptoms"], "text": "Pre-race anxiety has two distinguishable components: cognitive anxiety (worry thoughts about performance) and somatic anxiety (physical symptoms — heart rate, stomach distress, muscle tension). They respond to different interventions: cognitive anxiety is best managed with cognitive restructuring and attentional redirection; somatic anxiety responds to controlled breathing and progressive muscle relaxation. Psychologist annotations in race week should address both components, as runners often conflate the two and apply general 'calming' strategies that only address one."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_anxiety_PST_program_0079", "source_registry_id": "src_sport_psychology_psych_interventions_meta_2024", "source_file": "psychological_interventions_sport_performance_meta_2024.url", "source_path": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10933186/", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10933186/", "source_label": "Psychological interventions for sports performance systematic review and meta-analysis — PMC 2024", "quality_tier": "systematic_review_meta_analysis", "page": 3, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["PST", "psychological skills training", "marathon", "pre-race anxiety", "intervention"], "text": "Research has demonstrated the effectiveness of Psychological Skill Training (PST) programs to help amateur marathon runners manage excess pre-race anxiety and the disruptive negative thoughts that accompany it. A structured PST program for a 16-week marathon block includes: relaxation training (weeks 1–3), attentional skills (weeks 4–7), visualization (weeks 8–11), goal-setting refinement (weeks 12–14), and race simulation mental rehearsal (weeks 15–16). The psychologist node can frame its annotations as phase-aligned psychological skill development rather than one-off advice."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_anxiety_reappraisal_facilitating_0080", "source_registry_id": "src_prerace_anxiety_runners_guide_2025", "source_file": "prerace_anxiety_runners_guide_2025.url", "source_path": "https://runnersconnect.net/beat-pre-race-anxiety-the-runners-guide-to-managing-performance-nerves-2025/", "source_url": "https://runnersconnect.net/beat-pre-race-anxiety-the-runners-guide-to-managing-performance-nerves-2025/", "source_label": "Beat Pre Race Anxiety: The Runner's Guide to Managing Performance Nerves — Runners Connect 2025", "quality_tier": "practitioner_review", "page": 4, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["anxiety reappraisal", "facilitating", "performance", "arousal interpretation", "challenge response"], "text": "Research by Alison Wood Brooks (2014, Journal of Experimental Psychology) found that reappraising pre-performance anxiety as excitement ('I am excited') rather than attempting to calm down produced superior performance outcomes. The mechanism is arousal congruence: the physiological state of anxiety (high activation) is closer to excitement than to calm, making the transition to excitement more achievable and more motivating than suppression. For marathon runners, psychologist race-week scripts can include the reappraisal prompt: 'You are not nervous — you are ready.'"}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_anxiety_gender_differences_0081", "source_registry_id": "src_prerace_anxiety_runners_guide_2025", "source_file": "prerace_anxiety_runners_guide_2025.url", "source_path": "https://runnersconnect.net/beat-pre-race-anxiety-the-runners-guide-to-managing-performance-nerves-2025/", "source_url": "https://runnersconnect.net/beat-pre-race-anxiety-the-runners-guide-to-managing-performance-nerves-2025/", "source_label": "Beat Pre Race Anxiety: The Runner's Guide to Managing Performance Nerves — Runners Connect 2025", "quality_tier": "practitioner_review", "page": 4, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["gender", "anxiety", "women", "social support", "pre-race", "research"], "text": "Research consistently finds that female marathon runners report higher pre-race anxiety and greater reliance on social support coping strategies compared with male runners. Female runners may also benefit more from confidence-building visualization and social support activation strategies before races. The psychologist node should be aware of this evidence when interpreting runner self-reports — elevated anxiety in female runners is normative and does not necessarily indicate pathological distress, but it may call for more robust pre-race psychological preparation scaffolding."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_confidence_self_efficacy_sources_0082", "source_registry_id": "src_prerace_anxiety_runners_guide_2025", "source_file": "prerace_anxiety_runners_guide_2025.url", "source_path": "https://runnersconnect.net/beat-pre-race-anxiety-the-runners-guide-to-managing-performance-nerves-2025/", "source_url": "https://runnersconnect.net/beat-pre-race-anxiety-the-runners-guide-to-managing-performance-nerves-2025/", "source_label": "Beat Pre Race Anxiety: The Runner's Guide to Managing Performance Nerves — Runners Connect 2025", "quality_tier": "practitioner_review", "page": 5, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["self-efficacy", "confidence", "Bandura", "four sources", "performance accomplishments"], "text": "Bandura's theory identifies four sources of self-efficacy relevant to marathon running: (1) performance accomplishments — the most powerful source, based on the runner's own training history; (2) vicarious experience — observing similar others succeed; (3) verbal persuasion — encouragement from coaches or peers; (4) physiological interpretation — reading physical readiness signals correctly. The psychologist node draws primarily on source 1 (training history evidence) and source 4 (physiological interpretation reframing) in its pre-race confidence-building annotations."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_confidence_training_evidence_0083", "source_registry_id": "src_prerace_anxiety_runners_guide_2025", "source_file": "prerace_anxiety_runners_guide_2025.url", "source_path": "https://runnersconnect.net/beat-pre-race-anxiety-the-runners-guide-to-managing-performance-nerves-2025/", "source_url": "https://runnersconnect.net/beat-pre-race-anxiety-the-runners-guide-to-managing-performance-nerves-2025/", "source_label": "Beat Pre Race Anxiety: The Runner's Guide to Managing Performance Nerves — Runners Connect 2025", "quality_tier": "practitioner_review", "page": 5, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["training evidence", "confidence", "long run", "taper", "readiness assessment"], "text": "A structured training evidence review in race week — summarizing the runner's completed key sessions, long run distances, cumulative mileage, and any breakthrough training moments — is one of the most effective confidence-building interventions available to a marathon assistant's psychologist node. Research shows that runners who can explicitly recall evidence of their preparation enter race day with higher self-efficacy and lower anxiety. The psychologist annotation for race week should include a training evidence summary drawn from the runner's session history."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_boundary_clinical_referral_0084", "source_registry_id": "src_visualization_imagery_sport_performance_review", "source_file": "visualization_imagery_sport_performance_review.url", "source_path": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/344587632_Visualisation_techniques_in_sport_-_the_mental_road_map_for_success", "source_url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/344587632_Visualisation_techniques_in_sport_-_the_mental_road_map_for_success", "source_label": "Visualisation techniques in sport — the mental road map for success (ResearchGate review)", "quality_tier": "expert_review", "page": 5, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["clinical boundary", "referral", "mental health", "anxiety disorder", "sport psychologist"], "text": "The psychologist node in a marathon assistant operates strictly within the non-clinical sport psychology domain. Performance anxiety, race nerves, and motivational dips are within scope; persistent generalized anxiety, depression, disordered eating, or clinical-level psychological distress are outside scope and require referral to a qualified mental health professional. The IOC consensus statement on mental health in elite athletes (2019) frames athlete mental health as a safeguarding domain when symptoms are persistent, severe, or impairing daily function."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_confidence_comeback_after_poor_race_0085", "source_registry_id": "src_sport_psychology_psych_interventions_meta_2024", "source_file": "psychological_interventions_sport_performance_meta_2024.url", "source_path": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10933186/", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10933186/", "source_label": "Psychological interventions for sports performance systematic review and meta-analysis — PMC 2024", "quality_tier": "systematic_review_meta_analysis", "page": 4, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["recovery", "poor performance", "resilience", "growth mindset", "self-compassion"], "text": "Recovery from a poor marathon performance — a DNF, a missed time goal, or a race derailed by injury or illness — requires psychological recovery alongside physical recovery. Sport psychology recommendations include: (1) allowing a period of honest disappointment without self-attack; (2) analyzing what was within vs. outside control; (3) identifying what was learned; (4) reframing the experience in terms of long-term athletic development. Self-compassion (treating oneself as one would a respected friend who failed) is a more effective recovery strategy than self-criticism."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_race_execution_focus_plan_0086", "source_registry_id": "src_prerace_routine_psychological_effects_2025", "source_file": "prerace_routine_psychological_effects_2025.url", "source_path": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/395585347_Pre-Game_Routines_and_Their_Psychological_Effects_on_Performance_Consistency", "source_url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/395585347_Pre-Game_Routines_and_Their_Psychological_Effects_on_Performance_Consistency", "source_label": "Pre-Game Routines and Their Psychological Effects on Performance Consistency — ResearchGate 2025", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 3, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["race execution", "focus plan", "process focus", "distraction", "pre-race strategy"], "text": "A race focus plan — a pre-agreed structure for where to direct attention at each stage of the marathon — provides a cognitive anchor that prevents anxiety-driven attention drift on race day. Research on pre-performance routines confirms that athletes with explicit attention plans execute more consistently in competition than those without. A marathon focus plan typically specifies: start-line attention (breathing, posture, calm), first 10 km (controlled effort, resist surging), middle miles (process cues, form checks), final 10 km (effort-based attention, chunking, motivational self-talk)."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_race_phase_start_mental_0087", "source_registry_id": "src_prerace_routine_psychological_effects_2025", "source_file": "prerace_routine_psychological_effects_2025.url", "source_path": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/395585347_Pre-Game_Routines_and_Their_Psychological_Effects_on_Performance_Consistency", "source_url": "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/395585347_Pre-Game_Routines_and_Their_Psychological_Effects_on_Performance_Consistency", "source_label": "Pre-Game Routines and Their Psychological Effects on Performance Consistency — ResearchGate 2025", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 3, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["race start", "arousal", "crowd", "adrenaline", "pacing risk", "mental start"], "text": "The marathon start is the highest-risk zone for pacing errors driven by psychological arousal: crowd energy, adrenaline, and the relief of race day combine to push pace 10–20 seconds per kilometer faster than planned. Research on marathon pacing failures identifies the first 10 km as the most common site of the pace errors that cause mid-race collapse. The psychologist node's race-week annotation should include a specific mental preparation for the start: 'Feel the crowd energy and release it — your pace plan was written for your fitness, not the crowd's enthusiasm.'"}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_race_phase_halfway_mental_0088", "source_registry_id": "src_marathon_hitting_wall_mental_strategies_2026", "source_file": "marathon_hitting_wall_mental_strategies_2026.url", "source_path": "https://runlovers.it/en/2026/marathon-mental-strategies-hitting-the-wall/", "source_url": "https://runlovers.it/en/2026/marathon-mental-strategies-hitting-the-wall/", "source_label": "Marathon Mindset: Proven Strategies to Survive the Wall and Cross the Finish Line — Runlovers 2026", "quality_tier": "practitioner_review", "page": 6, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["halfway", "km 21", "mental check-in", "goal assessment", "positive split risk"], "text": "The halfway point of a marathon is the optimal location for a structured mental check-in: assessing perceived effort versus actual pace, adjusting goal expectations if needed, and resetting attentional focus for the second half. Runners who perform a deliberate halfway mental check-in — rather than a continuous stream of undifferentiated effort — report better emotional regulation in the final third. Psychologist race-week notes can include a simple halfway check-in protocol: 'At km 21: pace check, energy assessment, B-goal review, and 3-breath reset.'"}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_race_phase_km30_mental_0089", "source_registry_id": "src_marathon_hitting_wall_mental_strategies_2026", "source_file": "marathon_hitting_wall_mental_strategies_2026.url", "source_path": "https://runlovers.it/en/2026/marathon-mental-strategies-hitting-the-wall/", "source_url": "https://runlovers.it/en/2026/marathon-mental-strategies-hitting-the-wall/", "source_label": "Marathon Mindset: Proven Strategies to Survive the Wall and Cross the Finish Line — Runlovers 2026", "quality_tier": "practitioner_review", "page": 6, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["km 30", "mental tough point", "challenge zone", "resilience", "commitment"], "text": "Kilometer 30 in a marathon is consistently identified as the most psychologically demanding phase — glycogen stores are depleting, residual fatigue is accumulating, and the finish remains 12 km away. Research on the marathon wall experience identifies this zone as where dissociation strategies tend to fail and runners require a shift to more cognitively active coping. Psychological preparation notes should specifically address the km 25–35 zone with a distinct set of mental strategies: chunking, wall acceptance, social motivation, and prepared mantras."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_race_phase_final5km_mental_0090", "source_registry_id": "src_marathon_hitting_wall_mental_strategies_2026", "source_file": "marathon_hitting_wall_mental_strategies_2026.url", "source_path": "https://runlovers.it/en/2026/marathon-mental-strategies-hitting-the-wall/", "source_url": "https://runlovers.it/en/2026/marathon-mental-strategies-hitting-the-wall/", "source_label": "Marathon Mindset: Proven Strategies to Survive the Wall and Cross the Finish Line — Runlovers 2026", "quality_tier": "practitioner_review", "page": 7, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["final 5 km", "finish", "fatigue", "commitment cue", "emotional activation"], "text": "The final 5 km of a marathon presents a unique psychological dynamic: the finish is visible in concept, motivation often surges from proximity, and simultaneously fatigue is at its maximum. Sport psychology recommends transitioning from dissociative attention strategies to associative internal focus in the final 5 km to support accurate pace judgment, while using emotional activation cues (crowd noise, mental imagery of the finish line) to sustain effort. This final-phase attention shift should be explicitly rehearsed in visualization sessions before race day."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_weekly_psych_session_annotations_0091", "source_registry_id": "src_sport_psychology_psych_interventions_meta_2024", "source_file": "psychological_interventions_sport_performance_meta_2024.url", "source_path": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10933186/", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10933186/", "source_label": "Psychological interventions for sports performance systematic review and meta-analysis — PMC 2024", "quality_tier": "systematic_review_meta_analysis", "page": 5, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["weekly cycle", "annotations", "pre-workout", "post-workout", "psychology integration"], "text": "Effective integration of sport psychology into marathon training involves per-session annotation layers rather than isolated race-week preparation. Research on PST programs confirms that psychological skills — like physical fitness — develop through consistent practice across the training block. In a marathon assistant context, the psychologist node should provide: (1) pre-workout intentional focus prompts for key sessions; (2) brief post-workout reflection questions for long runs and interval sessions; (3) phase-aligned mental skill themes that evolve across the training plan macrocycle."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_training_block_periodization_mental_0092", "source_registry_id": "src_sport_psychology_psych_interventions_meta_2024", "source_file": "psychological_interventions_sport_performance_meta_2024.url", "source_path": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10933186/", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10933186/", "source_label": "Psychological interventions for sports performance systematic review and meta-analysis — PMC 2024", "quality_tier": "systematic_review_meta_analysis", "page": 5, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["mental periodization", "training block", "psychological skill development", "phase alignment"], "text": "Just as physical training is periodized across base, build, and peak phases, psychological skill development can be periodized to align with the physical demands of each training phase. Base phase: emphasis on motivation, identity, and goal clarity. Build phase: developing pain tolerance, self-talk skills, and attention strategies during increasing-intensity sessions. Peak phase: visualization, race-scenario rehearsal, anxiety management, and pre-race routine development. Taper phase: confidence maintenance, anxiety management, and race execution planning. This phase-aligned structure maximizes psychological readiness at race day."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_injury_mental_impact_0093", "source_registry_id": "src_pain_coping_ultra_endurance_pmc_2025", "source_file": "pain_coping_ultra_endurance_pmc_2025.url", "source_path": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12476407/", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12476407/", "source_label": "Human Resilience and Pain Coping Strategies — Elite Ultra-Endurance Athletes Review, PMC 2025", "quality_tier": "systematic_review_meta_analysis", "page": 4, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["injury psychology", "fear of re-injury", "confidence loss", "return to sport", "rehabilitation"], "text": "Injury interrupts marathon training not only physically but psychologically: research documents significant confidence loss, fear of re-injury, mood disturbance, and identity disruption in recreational runners following injury. Psychological recovery from injury should be considered a parallel process to physical rehabilitation. Key psychological tasks during recovery: maintaining training identity through adapted activity, using visualization to preserve motor representations, and gradually rebuilding confidence through progressive return-to-training milestones."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_sleep_performance_mental_0094", "source_registry_id": "src_motivation_overtraining_burnout_sdt_2007", "source_file": "motivation_overtraining_burnout_sdt_2007.url", "source_path": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17461390701302607", "source_url": "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/17461390701302607", "source_label": "Motivation, overtraining, and burnout: Can self-determined motivation predict overtraining and burnout in elite athletes? — Taylor & Francis 2007", "quality_tier": "peer_reviewed_study", "page": 5, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["sleep", "mental recovery", "overtraining", "mood", "HRV", "recovery monitoring"], "text": "Sleep quality is the primary driver of psychological recovery from training stress. Research shows that sleep deprivation impairs mood, motivation, pain tolerance, and self-regulation — the exact psychological capacities required for marathon training adherence and race execution. In training periods with elevated load, sleep monitoring (quality, duration) is as important as HRV for detecting emerging psychological overreach. Psychologist annotations in high-load weeks should include sleep quality prompts and provide evidence-based sleep hygiene guidance as preparation support."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_post_race_recovery_mental_0095", "source_registry_id": "src_sport_psychology_psych_interventions_meta_2024", "source_file": "psychological_interventions_sport_performance_meta_2024.url", "source_path": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10933186/", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10933186/", "source_label": "Psychological interventions for sports performance systematic review and meta-analysis — PMC 2024", "quality_tier": "systematic_review_meta_analysis", "page": 6, "paragraph_index": 1, "keywords_en": ["post-race", "psychological recovery", "post-marathon blues", "meaning", "future goals"], "text": "Post-marathon psychological recovery includes managing 'post-marathon blues' — a well-documented mood dip in the 1–4 weeks following a marathon, characterized by reduced motivation, mild low mood, and identity disruption. The phenomenon is partly physiological (hormonal and inflammatory) and partly psychological (loss of goal structure). Research-supported strategies: acknowledging the blues as normal and expected, maintaining light activity for physical mood regulation, and beginning to develop future goals to restore motivational direction. The psychologist node should flag post-race recovery as a distinct phase."}
{"domain_terms": ["sport_psychology"], "evidence_domain": "sport_psychology", "expert_domain": "sport_psychology", "domain_pack": "psychological_skills", "knowledge_layer": "document_index", "allowed_use": "explanation", "prescription_permission": "explanation_only", "exclude_from_training_generation": true, "display_mode": "verified_source", "evidence_source_type": "retrieval_evidence", "can_write_core": false, "explanation_only": true, "review_status": "approved", "needs_review": false, "language": "en", "section": "curated_summary", "has_full_text": true, "source_status": "ready", "chunk_id": "sport_psychology_psychological_skills_transfer_0096", "source_registry_id": "src_sport_psychology_psych_interventions_meta_2024", "source_file": "psychological_interventions_sport_performance_meta_2024.url", "source_path": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10933186/", "source_url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10933186/", "source_label": "Psychological interventions for sports performance systematic review and meta-analysis — PMC 2024", "quality_tier": "systematic_review_meta_analysis", "page": 6, "paragraph_index": 2, "keywords_en": ["psychological skills", "transfer", "life skills", "resilience", "broader wellbeing"], "text": "Research in sport psychology confirms that mental skills developed through marathon training — goal setting, self-talk, cognitive reappraisal, attentional focus, resilience — transfer meaningfully to non-sport life domains. Runners who explicitly recognize this transfer are more motivated to invest in mental skill development during training. Psychologist annotations can occasionally reference this broader value, particularly during difficult training phases: the psycho